var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#tutorial-first-rule","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"In this tutorial you test the two rules of a node with tables. You write a case for each rule, learn what a case checks, and read the report of a case that fails.\n\nYou need to know what a rule is and how one is defined. MessagePassingRulesBase's Your first node derives the rules this page tests.","title":"Testing your first rule"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#The-node","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"The node is a normal distribution with a known variance, f(y x v) = mathcalN(y mid x v), where y is the output out, x the mean μ and v the variance v:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end   # out ~ Normal(μ, v)\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\nThe line Gaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v) makes the node callable as the distribution of its output. The next tutorial uses it to check the rules against the node's density.","title":"The node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#A-belief-propagation-rule","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"The belief propagation message towards out integrates the factor against the message on μ. With a normal message of mean m and variance w on μ, and a known variance v, it is a normal of mean m and variance w + v:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\nThe integral is a normalised density, so the rule's log scale is 0.","title":"A belief propagation rule"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#A-table","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"A table lists cases. A case is the inputs of one call to the rule and the result the rule must return:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [\n            (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary: | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out | \u001b[32m  11  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   11  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.2s\n\nThe inputs are a NamedTuple. Its entry m holds the inbound messages, keyed by the interfaces' names. The table selects the rule from the inputs' types, as an engine would.\n\nThe summary counts eleven checks for the one case:\n\nThe rule is found. One rule takes these inputs.\nThe result is the expected one. The types must match exactly, a NormalMeanVariance{Float64} for a NormalMeanVariance{Float64}. The values must agree within the tolerances. approximately_equal makes the comparison.\nThe result has the promoted type. The case runs nine more times, with its inputs converted to Float32, Float64 and BigFloat: both inputs at once, then each alone. With both inputs in Float32, the result must be a NormalMeanVariance{Float32}.","title":"A table"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#The-log-scale","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"A plain expected value leaves the log scale unchecked. Wrap it in ExpectedWithLogScale to check the log scale the rule declares:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, with its log scale\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [\n            (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5), 0),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:                     | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, with its log scale | \u001b[32m  12  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   12  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.1s\n\nThe case makes one more check, the log scale's.","title":"The log scale"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#A-variational-rule","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"Under variational message passing, the rule towards out reads the marginal of μ, not its message. The message is a normal of the marginal's mean and the variance v:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\nA case for it gives marginals, in the entry q. One table holds the cases of both rules:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, both rules\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [\n            (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5), 0),\n            (q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:             | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, both rules | \u001b[32m  23  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   23  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.8s\n\nThe first case selects the belief propagation rule and the second the variational one, by the inputs each gives.","title":"A variational rule"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#A-failing-case","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"A rule can be right for Float64 inputs and still fail its table. This version of the variational rule converts the variance to Float64:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), Float64(mean(args.q[:v]))),\n)\n\nRun its case again. The page collects the failures and prints them, as Reading a failure shows. In your suite, each prints the same way, at the line of the table:\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out, variational\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5)],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_output ≈ expected\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(0.5))), with input(s) [1, 2] converted to Float32: got NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=0.5)::NormalMeanVariance{Float64}, expected NormalMeanVariance{Float32}(μ=1.0f0, v=0.5f0)::NormalMeanVariance{Float32}\n\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_output ≈ expected\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(0.5))), with input(s) [2] converted to BigFloat: got NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=0.5)::NormalMeanVariance{Float64}, expected NormalMeanVariance{BigFloat}(μ=1.0, v=0.5)::NormalMeanVariance{BigFloat}\n\nRead a report from the top:\n\nExpression names the check: rule_output ≈ expected compares the result with the expected one.\nEvaluated says which run failed: the case, the rule, the inputs, and which inputs were converted to which float type. Then it gives both values, each with its type.\n\nThe value is right in both reports, and the type is wrong. Both runs convert v: with every input in Float32, and with v alone in BigFloat. The result must follow v's type, and the rule's Float64(…) holds it at Float64. Remove the conversion, and the case passes:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, variational\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5)],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:              | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, variational | \u001b[32m  11  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   11  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.2s","title":"A failing case"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first-rule/#Next","page":"Testing your first rule","text":"The expected values in these tables are worked out by hand, so they can repeat a mistake of the rule's derivation. Testing a rule package checks the rules against the node's density instead, adds derivative checks, and makes the suite fail when a rule has no test. Table tests lists every keyword of a table.","title":"Next"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#Reading-a-failure","page":"Reading a failure","text":"Every check of this package is a Test assertion, reported at the line of the test that made it. A failing check prints two lines that matter:\n\nExpression names the check, such as rule_output ≈ expected or rule_found.\nEvaluated says which run failed and why: the case, the rule, the inputs, which inputs were converted to another float type, and the values that disagree.\n\nThis page shows each kind of failure and what to do about it. It tests one node, a normal distribution with a known variance:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end   # out ~ Normal(μ, v)\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)","title":"Reading a failure"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#Showing-failures-on-a-page","page":"Reading a failure","text":"A failing check inside a @testset makes the test set throw when it ends, which would stop this page. So the page runs its tests in a test set of its own, which records the results without reporting them. It prints the failures it collected, with the names of types shortened:\n\nstruct Collected <: Test.AbstractTestSet\n    description::String\n    results::Vector{Any}\nend\nCollected(description; kwargs...) = Collected(description, Any[])\nTest.record(ts::Collected, result) = (push!(ts.results, result); result)\nTest.finish(ts::Collected) = ts\n\n# The terminal's colours removed, and module-qualified names, `ExponentialFamily.NormalMeanVariance`,\n# shortened to the type's name.\nshorten(text) = replace(replace(text, r\"\\e\\[[0-9;]*m\" => \"\"), r\"(?:var\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\"\\.|\\b[A-Z]\\w*\\.|\\b__\\w+\\.)+(?=[A-Za-z_])\" => \"\")\n\nfunction failures(ts::Collected; limit = typemax(Int))\n    failed = filter(result -> result isa Test.Fail, ts.results)\n    for result in first(failed, limit)\n        println(\"Test Failed\\n  Expression: \", result.orig_expr, \"\\n   Evaluated: \", shorten(result.data), \"\\n\")\n    end\n    length(failed) > limit && println(\"… and \", length(failed) - limit, \" more\")\n    return nothing\nend\n\nIn your suite you need none of this. An ordinary @testset prints each failure the same way, with the file and the line of the test, and with each name qualified by its module, such as ExponentialFamily.NormalMeanVariance.","title":"Showing failures on a page"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#A-wrong-value","page":"Reading a failure","text":"The expected value of this case is wrong: the variance is 2.5, not 2.6.\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.6)],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts; limit = 1)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_output ≈ expected\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(0.5))): got NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.5)::NormalMeanVariance{Float64}, expected NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.6)::NormalMeanVariance{Float64}\n\n… and 9 more\n\nThe first report is the case on its own inputs. The others are its runs with the inputs converted to other float types, and they fail the same way.\n\nWhat to do. When every run of a case fails, the value is wrong, in the rule or in the table. Work the expected value out again, or check the rule against the node's density with @verify_message_update_rule, which needs no expected value.","title":"A wrong value"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#A-promoted-type","page":"Reading a failure","text":"When the case on its own inputs passes and only converted runs fail, the result has the wrong type. This average energy multiplies by the constant 2π, a Float64:\n\n@define_average_energy(\n    node = Gaussian,\n    args = (q[:out]::Any, q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> begin\n        y, x, v = args.q[:out], args.q[:μ], mean(args.q[:v])\n        (log(2π * v) + (var(y) + var(x) + (mean(y) - mean(x))^2) / v) / 2\n    end,\n)\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: average energy\" begin\n    @test_average_energy(\n        node = Gaussian,\n        cases = [(q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(1.0)),) => 1.5 + log(2π) / 2],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_output ≈ expected\n   Evaluated: case 1, average_energy rule for Gaussian under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (q = (out = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=0.0, v=1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=1.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(1.0))), with input(s) [1, 2, 3] converted to Float32: got 2.4189385332046727::Float64, expected 2.4189386f0::Float32\n\nWith every input in Float32, the energy must be a Float32. The product 2π * v is a Float64, and it makes the whole energy one.\n\nWhat to do. Find the value that does not follow the inputs' type. A Float64 constant promotes everything it touches: write 2v * π, whose π takes the type of 2v, or convert the constant with oftype. A literal such as 0.5 does the same; write v / 2 instead of 0.5v. An explicit Float64(…) holds the result at Float64, as Testing your first rule shows.","title":"A promoted type"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#No-rule-for-the-inputs","page":"Reading a failure","text":"The case gives the variance as a number, where the rule takes a PointMass:\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = 0.5),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5)],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_found\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.0), v = 0.5)): RuleNotFoundError: no message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm() takes the inputs (m[:v]::Float64, m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance{Float64})\n  a rule of this shape exists, but the input types do not fit (type mismatch)\n  what to try: the inputs arrive with other types than the rule takes. If a rule below takes such an input as a marginal `q`, a factorisation that separates it delivers one (in RxInfer, `@constraints`); a form constraint on a variable projects its marginal onto a family a rule takes; otherwise, define a rule for these types\n  near misses:\n    rule at failures.md:22\n      ✓ algorithm DefaultAlgorithm\n      ✓ m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance  got NormalMeanVariance{Float64}\n      ✗ m[:v]::PointMass  got Float64\n\nThe check is rule_found. The report is the base package's RuleNotFoundError: it lists the rules close to the inputs, with a ✓ for each input that fits and a ✗ for each that does not.\n\nWhat to do. Read the ✗ lines. The case may give a message where the rule takes a marginal, m for q, or an input under the wrong name, or of another type. A rule for those inputs may also be missing, and then the table found a real gap.","title":"No rule for the inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#The-rule-throws","page":"Reading a failure","text":"A sign error makes this rule's variance negative, and sqrt throws:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :μ,\n    args = (m[:out]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> Normal(mean(args.m[:out]), sqrt(var(args.m[:out]) - mean(args.m[:v]))),\n)\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: μ\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :μ,\n        cases = [(m = (out = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => Normal(1.0, sqrt(2.5))],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts; limit = 1)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: rule_runs\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :μ under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (m = (out = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=0.5), v = PointMass{Float64}(2.0))): the rule threw DomainError with -1.5:\nsqrt was called with a negative real argument but will only return a complex result if called with a complex argument. Try sqrt(Complex(x)).\n    at throw_complex_domainerror(f::Symbol, x::Float64) at math.jl:33\n    at sqrt at math.jl:645 [inlined]\n    at #26 at failures.md:151 [inlined]\n    at #28 at rule_macro.jl:528 [inlined]\n    at execute_rule_body at rulespec.jl:301 [inlined]\n    at execute_rule_with_logscale at rulespec.jl:288 [inlined]\n\nThe check is rule_runs. The report gives the exception and the frames it was thrown from, the rule's body first. The table goes on with its other cases and runs.\n\nWhat to do. Read the exception and the first frame. Here the variance is a sum, not a difference.","title":"The rule throws"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#A-log-scale","page":"Reading a failure","text":"ExpectedWithLogScale checks the log scale a rule declares. The rule towards out declares 0, and this case expects -1:\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out, log scale\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5), -1)],\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: logscale ≈ expected\n   Evaluated: case 1, message rule for Gaussian towards :out under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=2.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(0.5))): the log scale is 0, expected -1\n\nThe check is logscale ≈ expected. A computed log scale must also follow the inputs' float type, like the result. A log scale of the wrong type fails with a report that names both types.\n\nWhat to do. @verify_message_update_rule computes the log scale a belief propagation message must have, from the node's density, and reports it when it differs from the declared one.","title":"A log scale"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#A-verification","page":"Reading a failure","text":"Verification reports two checks. shape_matches_node_definition fails when the message has the wrong shape: the log-ratio of the message to the integral of the node's density varies over the test points. Testing a rule package shows one. logscale_matches_node_definition fails when the shape is right and the declared log scale is not. This rule declares 1, where the density implies 0:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::PointMass, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 1,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out, verified\" begin\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: logscale_matches_node_definition\n   Evaluated: belief propagation message target :out: the node definition implies log scale -0.0, the rule declares 1\n\nWhat to do. Trust the integral over the hand derivation, after checking that the node's density, nodefunction, is the one the rule assumes. Verification against the node lists the inputs verification can integrate.","title":"A verification"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#A-derivative","page":"Reading a failure","text":"@test_rule_derivatives reports two kinds of failure:\n\nautomatic_derivative ≈ finite_difference: the rule runs with ForwardDiff's dual numbers, but its derivative differs from a central finite difference. The report gives both derivatives.\nrule_runs, labelled with the derivative it was computing: the rule throws on dual numbers. The usual cause is a conversion, Float64(x), which has no method for a dual number. The report gives the exception and the frames, whose types spell out the dual numbers in full.\n\nWhat to do. Remove the conversion, or write it with the input's own type, float(x). A wrong derivative from a rule that runs usually means a value computed outside the rule's inputs, such as a cached one.","title":"A derivative"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#An-allocation","page":"Reading a failure","text":"With check_nonallocating = true, a case also measures the rule's call through the engine's entry point, which must allocate nothing. This average energy computes the difference of the means as vectors, the way a rule for vectors would:\n\n@define_average_energy(\n    node = Gaussian,\n    args = (q[:out]::Any, q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> begin\n        y, x, v = args.q[:out], args.q[:μ], mean(args.q[:v])\n        d = [mean(y)] .- [mean(x)]\n        (log(2v * π) + (var(y) + var(x) + sum(abs2, d)) / v) / 2\n    end,\n)\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: average energy, allocations\" begin\n    @test_average_energy(\n        node = Gaussian,\n        cases = [(q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(1.0)),) => 1.5 + log(2π) / 2],\n        check_nonallocating = true,\n    )\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: allocated_bytes == 0\n   Evaluated: case 1, average_energy rule for Gaussian under DefaultAlgorithm(), inputs (q = (out = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=0.0, v=1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance{Float64}(μ=1.0, v=1.0), v = PointMass{Float64}(1.0))): allocated 192 bytes\n\nThe check is allocated_bytes == 0.\n\nWhat to do. Compute with numbers, mean(y) - mean(x), or with tuples, which do not live on the heap. A rule for vectors writes into a buffer it is given: an in-place rule into its output, or a rule with scratch into its scratch. Julia removes some temporary arrays itself, so a rule may pass this check on one Julia version and fail it on another.","title":"An allocation"},{"category":"section","location":"failures/#Other-checks","page":"Reading a failure","text":"Expression What failed What to do\nrule!(buffer) === buffer An in-place rule returned a new object instead of writing into the buffer from its preallocate. Write into the buffer and return it.\nrule! == rule The in-place rule and the plain one disagree. Check that the in-place body overwrites every entry of the buffer.\nrule_with_reused_scratch == rule A rule with scratch read its scratch before writing it: the result changed after poison! filled it with NaN. Write every entry of the scratch before reading it.\nannotation == expected A rule wrote another annotation than ExpectedWithAnnotations expects, or none. Check the annotate! calls of the body.\n\nThe rule-coverage gate fails one check, isempty(gaps), and prints a RuleCoverageGap for each rule no test selected, with the line that defined the rule. Add a case for that rule, or a verification. The rule-coverage gate says what counts.","title":"Other checks"},{"category":"section","location":"verification/#Verification-against-the-node","page":"Verification against the node","text":"A table's expected values are derived by hand. Verification checks a message rule against what it is derived from, the node's density. The message a rule computes must be, up to a constant, the node's factor integrated against its other inputs. Verification computes that integral numerically at a set of points.\n\nWith messages as inputs, the reference is belief propagation:\n\nlog mu(x) = log int f(x y_1 dots y_n) prod_j m_j(y_j)  dy + textconst\n\nThe constant is the rule's log scale: the message times the exponential of its log scale is the integral itself. With marginals, the reference is naive variational message passing, log mu(x) = mathrmE_qlog f(x y) + textconst.","title":"Verification against the node"},{"category":"section","location":"verification/#Verify-a-rule","page":"Verification against the node","text":"The node is the Gaussian of the first tutorial, f(y x v) = mathcalN(y mid x v). Verification reads its log-density from nodefunction, which the declaration defines when the node is callable as the distribution of its output: here Gaussian(μ, v) is a NormalMeanVariance.\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\nMessagePassingRulesBase.nodefunction(Gaussian)(out = 1.0, μ = 0.0, v = 2.0)\n-1.5155121234846454\n\nThe last line evaluates the log-density at y = 1, x = 0 and v = 2.\n\nVerify the belief propagation rule towards out, with a normal message on μ, which verification integrates:\n\n@verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.76081388253624e-11\n 7.105427357601002e-13\n 5.995204332975845e-14\n 7.771561172376096e-15\n 1.9984014443252818e-15\n 7.771561172376096e-15\n 5.995204332975845e-14\n 7.105427357601002e-13\n 3.76081388253624e-11\n\nThe result is the log-ratio of the message to the reference at nine quantiles of the message. It varies by less than atol, 1e-6 by default, so the shape is right. It is zero, which is minus the declared log scale, so the scale is right too.\n\nVerify the variational rule towards out, with marginals:\n\n@verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.37499996632001853\n 0.37499996805684277\n 0.37499996866824437\n 0.37499996894385657\n 0.37499996902556143\n 0.3749999689438568\n 0.3749999686682448\n 0.37499996805684366\n 0.37499996632001764\n\nThe log-ratio is again constant, so the shape is right. It is not zero, and nothing checks it: a variational message has no log scale, and this rule declares none.\n\nEach check is a Test assertion. Inside a @testset, the summary counts them:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: verification\" begin\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:          | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: verification | \u001b[32m   3  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m    3  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.0s\n\nWhen a check fails, the report gives the log-ratio at every point, or the log scale the node's density implies. Reading a failure shows both.","title":"Verify a rule"},{"category":"section","location":"verification/#Limitations","page":"Verification against the node","text":"The node must be stochastic and without groups: the reference is its nodefunction.\nThe inputs are point masses, which are substituted, finite discrete distributions, which are enumerated, and at most two continuous univariate distributions, which are integrated.\nThe message is univariate. It is compared at its quantiles, or over its support when it is discrete.\nA case takes messages or marginals, not both. A rule that mixes them is checked by its table only.","title":"Limitations"},{"category":"section","location":"verification/#Verify-any-implementation","page":"Verification against the node","text":"verify_message_update runs the same check on any function that returns a message and its log scale, for an implementation that is not a rule of the base package. It takes the node's log-density, its interface names and the target:\n\nmessage(m, q) = (NormalMeanVariance(mean(m.μ), var(m.μ) + mean(m.v)), 0.0)\nlogdensity(; out, μ, v) = logpdf(NormalMeanVariance(μ, v), out)\n\nverify_message_update(message, logdensity, (:out, :μ, :v), :out; m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.76081388253624e-11\n 7.105427357601002e-13\n 5.995204332975845e-14\n 7.771561172376096e-15\n 1.9984014443252818e-15\n 7.771561172376096e-15\n 5.995204332975845e-14\n 7.105427357601002e-13\n 3.76081388253624e-11","title":"Verify any implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"verification/#API","page":"Verification against the node","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"verification/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@verify_message_update_rule","page":"Verification against the node","text":"@verify_message_update_rule(node = ..., target = ..., m = (...), q = (...), ...)\n\nCheck the message rule of a node towards one of its interfaces against the node's definition, integrated numerically. It takes the arguments and keywords of verify_message_update_rule, where the checks and their limits are described, all by name, and returns what it returns.\n\nThe macro takes keyword arguments only, written name = value, and passes them to verify_message_update_rule; node and target are required, and a positional argument or a missing required keyword is an error when the macro expands. Every check is reported against the line of the macro call, so a failure points at the call that made it.\n\nExamples\n\n@verify_message_update_rule(node = NormalMeanVariance, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@verify_message_update_rule"},{"category":"function","location":"verification/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.verify_message_update_rule","page":"Verification against the node","text":"verify_message_update_rule(node, target::Symbol; m = (;), q = (;), algorithm = default_algorithm(node),\n    points = nothing, atol = 1e-6) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCheck the message rule of node towards target against the node's definition: the message it returns, and the log scale it declares, against its nodefunction, integrated numerically. @verify_message_update_rule is the same check written with keywords, and reports failures at its own line. The rule is recorded as selected for check_rule_coverage.\n\nThe reference depends on the kind of inputs, which is one or the other:\n\nwith messages, m, belief propagation: log int f(x y) prod_j m_j(y_j)  dy, the node's density f integrated against the normalised inbound messages;\nwith marginals, q, naive variational message passing: mathrmE_qlog f(x y).\n\nEach input is treated by its kind: a PointMass is substituted; a discrete univariate distribution with fewer than 10 000 values in its support is enumerated; a continuous univariate distribution is integrated by HCubature between its 1e-9 and 1 - 1e-9 quantiles, for at most two such inputs per call.\n\nTwo checks are made, each a Test assertion:\n\nshape: logpdf(message, x) - reference(x) is the same at every test point, within atol, so the message is the reference up to a constant;\nscale, for belief propagation when the message comes with a log scale: that constant is -logscale, within atol, so the message times exp(logscale) is the reference itself. It is checked only when the shape holds, since otherwise there is no single constant to compare.\n\nThe rule's log scale is checked when it declares one; an undefined log scale leaves the scale unchecked.\n\nLimitations: the node must be stochastic and without groups, since only such a node has a nodefunction; target is a single interface, not a group member; the message must be a univariate distribution; the rule runs with an empty RuleContext, so a rule that reads services gets nothing for them; and a rule that takes messages and marginals together cannot be verified, since the inputs are one kind or the other. Such rules are checked by their tables.\n\nArguments\n\nnode: the node, as declared with @define_factor_node.\ntarget: the interface the message goes to, a Symbol.\n\nKeywords\n\nm: the inbound messages, a NamedTuple keyed by interface name, with one entry for every interface but target. Default: none.\nq: the marginals, keyed the same way. Default: none. Give m or q, not both.\npoints: the values of the target at which to compare. Default: nothing, which is the support of a discrete message, or 9 quantiles of the message, from 0.05 to 0.95.\natol: the absolute tolerance on the log-ratio's spread and on the log scale. Default: 1e-6.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\nalgorithm: the algorithm value to run under. Default: default_algorithm(node).\n\nReturns\n\nThe log-ratio logpdf(message, x) - reference(x) at each test point, in order; empty when no rule takes the inputs or the rule throws, each recorded as a failure, rule_found or rule_runs.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when both m and q are given, when an interface other than target has no input, when an input is neither a point mass nor a univariate distribution, when a discrete input's support has 10 000 values or more, or when more than two inputs need integrating.\nMethodError when node has no nodefunction.\n\nExamples\n\n@verify_message_update_rule(node = NormalMeanVariance, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.5, 1.5), v = PointMass(2.0)))\n@verify_message_update_rule(node = NormalMeanVariance, target = :μ, q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = InverseGamma(3.0, 4.0)))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.verify_message_update_rule"},{"category":"function","location":"verification/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.verify_message_update","page":"Verification against the node","text":"verify_message_update(message, logdensity, interfaces, target::Symbol; m = (;), q = (;),\n    points = nothing, atol = 1e-6) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCheck a message against the node definition it comes from, for any implementation of a rule: the message must be, up to a constant, the reference computed numerically from the node's log-density. verify_message_update_rule does this for a rule defined with the base package.\n\nThe reference depends on the kind of inputs, which is one or the other:\n\nwith messages, m, belief propagation: log int f(x y) prod_j m_j(y_j)  dy, the node's density f integrated against the normalised inbound messages;\nwith marginals, q, naive variational message passing: mathrmE_qlog f(x y).\n\nEach input is treated by its kind: a PointMass is substituted; a discrete univariate distribution with fewer than 10 000 values in its support is enumerated; a continuous univariate distribution is integrated by HCubature between its 1e-9 and 1 - 1e-9 quantiles, for at most two such inputs per call.\n\nTwo checks are made, each a Test assertion:\n\nshape: logpdf(message, x) - reference(x) is the same at every test point, within atol, so the message is the reference up to a constant;\nscale, for belief propagation when the message comes with a log scale: that constant is -logscale, within atol, so the message times exp(logscale) is the reference itself. It is checked only when the shape holds, since otherwise there is no single constant to compare.\n\nArguments\n\nmessage: a function (m, q) -> (message, logscale), called once with the keywords m and q, returning the outbound message, a univariate distribution, and its log scale, a number, or nothing to leave the scale unchecked. If it throws, the check fails with the exception, as rule_found for a missing rule and rule_runs otherwise.\nlogdensity: the node's log-density, called with one keyword per interface: logdensity(; out, μ, σ).\ninterfaces: the node's interface names, target among them.\ntarget: the interface the message goes to.\n\nKeywords\n\nm: the inbound messages, a NamedTuple keyed by interface name, with one entry for every interface but target. Default: none.\nq: the marginals, keyed the same way. Default: none. Give m or q, not both.\npoints: the values of the target at which to compare. Default: nothing, which is the support of a discrete message, or 9 quantiles of the message, from 0.05 to 0.95.\natol: the absolute tolerance on the log-ratio's spread and on the log scale. Default: 1e-6.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\n\nReturns\n\nThe log-ratio logpdf(message, x) - reference(x) at each test point, in order; empty when the message could not be computed, which is recorded as a failure.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when both m and q are given, when an interface other than target has no input, when an input is neither a point mass nor a univariate distribution, when a discrete input's support has 10 000 values or more, or when more than two inputs need integrating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.verify_message_update"},{"category":"section","location":"coverage/#The-rule-coverage-gate","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"A rule package ends its suite with a gate. After every test item has run, each rule the package defines must have been selected by some test, and each node must have some rule selected. A rule added without a test fails the suite.\n\nA rule counts as tested only when a test selected it, that is, resolved it as the rule to run. A test selects a rule through:\n\na table case, @test_message_update_rule and its siblings;\na verification, @verify_message_update_rule;\na derivative check, @test_rule_derivatives;\na direct call, call_message_update_rule, its siblings or their macros.","title":"The rule-coverage gate"},{"category":"section","location":"coverage/#Run-the-gate","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"The Gaussian node of the first tutorial has three rules here, and no test has run yet. check_rule_coverage takes the modules to check, here the page's own, and returns a RuleCoverageGap for each rule and node that no test exercised:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :μ,\n    args = (m[:out]::PointMass, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:out]), mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\nforeach(println, check_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :out under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ coverage.md:33))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :out under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ coverage.md:40))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :μ under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ coverage.md:46))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test exercises any rule of Gaussian)\n\nEach gap names a rule by its target, its algorithm and the line that defined it. The last gap is the node's.\n\nA table case selects the belief propagation rule towards out, and a direct call selects the variational one:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5)],\n    )\nend\n\nvmp = @call_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\n\nforeach(println, check_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__))\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary: | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out | \u001b[32m  11  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   11  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m1.3s\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :μ under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ coverage.md:46))\n\nThe node has a selected rule, so its gap is gone. The rule towards μ is still reported. A verification selects it, and the gate passes:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: μ\" begin\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :μ, m = (out = PointMass(3.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\nend\n\ncheck_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__)\nMessagePassingRulesTestUtils.RuleCoverageGap[]\n\nrule_test_locations says where the tests that selected a rule were written. A direct call has no source line:\n\nMessagePassingRulesTestUtils.rule_test_locations(getrule(vmp))\n1-element Vector{LineNumberNode}:\n :(\u001b[90m#= a direct call:0 =#\u001b[39m)","title":"Run the gate"},{"category":"section","location":"coverage/#What-does-not-count","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"A broader rule that would also have answered a case stays reported, so every method of a rule needs a case of its own. A rule reached only through a graph is not counted, since an engine resolves rules itself.","title":"What does not count"},{"category":"section","location":"coverage/#Wire-the-gate-into-a-suite","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"Selections accumulate over the whole process, from the moment the package is loaded. The gate means something only after an unfiltered run. So test/runtests.jl notes whether its filter left any test item out, and checks the gate only when none was. The docstring of check_rule_coverage below shows that file. A suite that tags items :slow and skips them by default runs the gate only with them, TEST_ALL=true.\n\nA package extension registers its rules in its own module. Give the gate that module as well: check_rule_coverage(MyRules, Base.get_extension(MyRules, :MyRulesExt)).","title":"Wire the gate into a suite"},{"category":"section","location":"coverage/#API","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"coverage/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.check_rule_coverage","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"check_rule_coverage(modules...) -> Vector{RuleCoverageGap}\n\nList the rules and nodes defined in modules that no test exercised, as RuleCoverageGaps; an empty list means every rule was tested.\n\nA rule counts as tested only when some test selected it, that is resolved it as the rule to run: a table case, a verification (verify_message_update_rule), a derivative check (test_rule_derivatives), or a direct call through call_message_update_rule, its siblings or their macros. A broader rule that would also have answered stays reported, and so does a rule reached only through a graph, since an engine resolves rules itself. A node counts as tested when any of its rules in modules was selected.\n\nSelections accumulate in the process from the moment the package is loaded and are never reset, so the gate means something only after the whole suite has run in one process, with nothing filtered out. A package's test/runtests.jl calls it after TestItemRunner's @run_package_tests, and only when no test item was left out:\n\n# `is_selected(ti)` is the suite's own selection, from `ARGS`.\nconst FILTERED_OUT = Ref(false)\ntest_item_filter(ti) = is_selected(ti) || (FILTERED_OUT[] = true; false)\n\n@run_package_tests(filter = test_item_filter)\n\nif !FILTERED_OUT[]\n    @testset \"rule coverage\" begin\n        gaps = check_rule_coverage(MyRules)\n        foreach(println, gaps)\n        @test isempty(gaps)\n    end\nend\n\nArguments\n\nmodules: the modules whose rules and nodes to check, such as the package and its extensions; with none, every loaded module.\n\nSee also rule_test_locations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.check_rule_coverage"},{"category":"type","location":"coverage/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.RuleCoverageGap","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"RuleCoverageGap\n\nSomething check_rule_coverage found untested: a rule that no test selected, or a node none of whose rules any test selected. subject is the rule's RuleSpec or the node's NodeSpec; message says what is missing, as in \"no test selects …\", and is what the gap prints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.RuleCoverageGap"},{"category":"function","location":"coverage/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.rule_test_locations","page":"The rule-coverage gate","text":"rule_test_locations(spec) -> Vector{LineNumberNode}\n\nWhere the tests that selected the rule spec were written: one LineNumberNode per selection, from a table case, a verification or a derivative check, in the order they ran. A direct call through call_message_update_rule and its siblings has no source line and appears as LineNumberNode(0, Symbol(\"a direct call\")). Empty when no test selected spec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.rule_test_locations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#tutorial-rule-package","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"In this tutorial you test a node's rules the way a rule package's suite does. You check a rule against the node's density, check its derivatives, and end the suite with a gate that fails when a rule has no test.\n\nIt continues Testing your first rule, with the same node.","title":"Testing a rule package"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#What-a-suite-holds","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"A rule package's suite has four parts:\n\nA table for every rule, as in the first tutorial: the result each case must return.\nVerification against the node: a message rule's result against the node's density, integrated numerically.\nDerivative checks, for rules a model differentiates through.\nThe rule-coverage gate, run after everything else: every rule the package defines must have been selected by some test.\n\nThe tests live in test/, one file of test items per node, and test/runtests.jl runs them and then the gate:\n\nMyRules/\n├── src/MyRules.jl\n└── test/\n    ├── runtests.jl          the test items, then the gate\n    └── gaussian_tests.jl    the Gaussian node's test items\n\nEach check is a Test assertion, so the tools go inside a @testitem or a @testset. This page uses @testset.","title":"What a suite holds"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#The-node","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"The node is the first tutorial's, a normal distribution with a known variance:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end   # out ~ Normal(μ, v)\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])","title":"The node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#Check-a-rule-against-the-node","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"A table's expected value is worked out by hand, from the same derivation as the rule. A mistake in the derivation then appears in both, and the table passes. Here the rule towards out forgets the variance of the message on μ, and so does its table:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5)],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary: | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out | \u001b[32m  11  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   11  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.1s\n\n@verify_message_update_rule does not take an expected value. It integrates the node's log-density, nodefunction, against the inputs, and compares the rule's message with that integral at a set of points. The failures are collected and printed, as in the first tutorial:\n\nts = @testset Collected \"Gaussian: out, verified\" begin\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\nend\nfailures(ts)\nTest Failed\n  Expression: shape_matches_node_definition\n   Evaluated: belief propagation message target :out: log-ratio to the node definition varies by 1.0822173816381664 over the test points; differences [-0.27749842542111613, 0.41723157327113847, 0.6617920889025224, 0.7720369539159182, 0.8047189562170503, 0.7720369539159183, 0.6617920889025224, 0.4172315732711387, -0.2774984254211148]\n\nThe log-ratio of the message to the integral must be the same at every point, since a message is defined up to a constant. Here it varies, so the message has the wrong shape. The log scale is not checked, since it means nothing for a message of the wrong shape.\n\nWith the variance of μ added, the rule passes both checks: the shape, and the log scale 0 it declares:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, verified\" begin\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :out, m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:           | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, verified | \u001b[32m   2  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m    2  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.1s\n\nVerification needs a stochastic node whose density it can integrate. Verification against the node lists the inputs it handles.","title":"Check a rule against the node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#Check-derivatives","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"A model may be differentiated, to fit a hyperparameter or to run a gradient-based sampler. The derivatives then pass through its rules. @test_rule_derivatives builds the rule's inputs from a parameter θ, reduces its result to a number with summary, and compares ForwardDiff's derivative with a finite difference:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, derivatives\" begin\n    @test_rule_derivatives(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        inputs = θ -> (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(θ, 1.0), v = PointMass(θ^2)),),\n        at = 1.5, summary = d -> mean(d) + var(d),\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:              | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, derivatives | \u001b[32m   1  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m    1  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.2s\n\nA rule that converts its inputs to Float64 drops ForwardDiff's dual numbers and fails here. Derivatives says more.","title":"Check derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#Gate-the-suite-on-coverage","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"The node gains two more rules, the variational message towards out and the belief propagation message towards μ from an observed output:\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :μ,\n    args = (m[:out]::PointMass, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:out]), mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\nNo test selects them yet. check_rule_coverage lists every rule of the given modules that no test selected. Here the module is the page's own:\n\nforeach(println, check_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :out under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ rule-package.md:157))\nRuleCoverageGap(no test selects RuleSpec(message rule for Gaussian towards :μ under MessagePassingRulesBase.DefaultAlgorithm @ rule-package.md:163))\n\nA rule counts once a test resolves it as the rule to run. A table case does, and so does a verification:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: the other rules\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 0.5)],\n    )\n    @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gaussian, target = :μ, m = (out = PointMass(3.0), v = PointMass(0.5)))\nend\n\ncheck_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__)\nMessagePassingRulesTestUtils.RuleCoverageGap[]\n\nThe list is empty, so every rule has a test.","title":"Gate the suite on coverage"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/rule-package/#Run-the-gate-in-a-suite","page":"Testing a rule package","text":"Selections add up over the whole process, so the gate means something only after the whole suite has run. test/runtests.jl therefore runs it last, and only when no test item was filtered out. The docstring of check_rule_coverage shows that file.\n\nWhen the gate fails, it prints each gap and fails one check. A new rule without a test then fails the package's suite, and its report names the rule and the line that defined it. The rule-coverage gate says what counts as a test, and what does not.","title":"Run the gate in a suite"},{"category":"section","location":"tables/#Table-tests","page":"Table tests","text":"A table lists cases. Each case is the inputs of one call to a rule and the result the rule must return. A table is the main test of every rule: it is cheap to write, it takes one line per case, and it checks each case in several ways at once.\n\nThis page tests the Gaussian node of the first tutorial, a normal distribution with a known variance, with a marginal rule and an average energy added:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.q[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])),\n)\n\n# The joint of `out` and `μ` under q(y, x) q(v), in the information form.\n@define_marginal_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = (:out, :μ),\n    args = (m[:out]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> begin\n        (my, vy), (mx, vx), v = mean_var(args.m[:out]), mean_var(args.m[:μ]), mean(args.q[:v])\n        MvNormalWeightedMeanPrecision([my / vy, mx / vx], [1 / vy + 1 / v -1 / v; -1 / v 1 / vx + 1 / v])\n    end,\n)\n\n@define_average_energy(\n    node = Gaussian,\n    args = (q[:out]::Any, q[:μ]::Any, q[:v]::PointMass),\n    body = (args) -> begin\n        y, x, v = args.q[:out], args.q[:μ], mean(args.q[:v])\n        (log(2v * π) + (var(y) + var(x) + (mean(y) - mean(x))^2) / v) / 2\n    end,\n)\n\nA table for the message rules towards out:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [\n            (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(3.0)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 5.0),\n            (q = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary: | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out | \u001b[32m  22  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   22  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m1.1s\n\nThe first case gives messages and selects the belief propagation rule. The second gives marginals and selects the variational one. Each case makes several checks, which the summary counts.","title":"Table tests"},{"category":"section","location":"tables/#Cases","page":"Table tests","text":"A case is inputs => expected. The inputs are a NamedTuple with any of these entries:\n\nm, the inbound messages, keyed by the interfaces' declared names.\nq, the marginals of single interfaces, keyed the same way. A group is a tuple of its members in order, with nothing where the rule does not take a member: m = (T = (PointMass(2.0), nothing),).\nclusters, the joint marginals of clusters, as pairs from the members to the joint: clusters = ((:out, :μ) => q_outμ,).\nctx, the RuleContext of services a rule reads, such as a random number generator. The table does not check its services.\nlogscale, the log scales that arrived with the messages, for a rule declared with reads_logscale = true, such as a mixture's: logscale = (in = 0.5,).\n\nThe expected value is the rule's result. approximately_equal compares it with what the rule returns. The type must match exactly, a Normal{Float64} for a Normal{Float64}, and the values must agree within the tolerances.\n\nTwo wrappers check more than the result. An ExpectedWithLogScale checks the log scale a message rule declares as well. An ExpectedWithAnnotations checks the annotations a rule writes.\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out, with its log scale\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [\n            (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 3.0), 0),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:                     | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out, with its log scale | \u001b[32m  12  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   12  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.1s","title":"Cases"},{"category":"section","location":"tables/#Marginal-rules-and-average-energies","page":"Table tests","text":"A marginal rule's table names a cluster as its target. This one expects the joint of out and μ:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: (out, μ)\" begin\n    @test_marginal_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = (:out, :μ),\n        cases = [\n            (m = (out = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 2.0)), q = (v = PointMass(0.5),)) =>\n                MvNormalWeightedMeanPrecision([1.0, 0.0], [3.0 -2.0; -2.0 2.5]),\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:      | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: (out, μ) | \u001b[32m  14  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   14  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m2.6s\n\nAn average energy's table has no target, and it expects a number:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: average energy\" begin\n    @test_average_energy(\n        node = Gaussian,\n        cases = [\n            (q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(1.0)),) => 1.5 + log(2π) / 2,\n        ],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:            | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: average energy | \u001b[32m  14  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   14  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.9s","title":"Marginal rules and average energies"},{"category":"section","location":"tables/#What-every-case-checks","page":"Table tests","text":"Beyond the result itself, each case checks:\n\nFloat-type promotion. The case runs again with its inputs converted to Float32, Float64 and BigFloat, the float_types. It converts all the inputs at once, then each alone. With check_type_promotion = :exhaustive, it converts every subset. The result must have the promoted type. A rule that hard-codes Float64 fails, and so does a computed log scale that ignores the inputs' precision.\nIn-place rules. A rule declared inplace = true runs again through rule!, into a buffer from its preallocate. It must write into that buffer, and the result must agree.\nScratch. A rule declared with scratch runs again on a scratch that poison! filled with NaN. It must give the same result, so a rule that reads its scratch before writing it fails.\nAllocations. With check_nonallocating = true, the rule's call through the engine's entry point must allocate nothing.\n\nThe same average-energy case makes more checks when every subset of its three inputs is converted and its allocations are measured. Compare the counts with the table above:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: average energy, exhaustively\" begin\n    @test_average_energy(\n        node = Gaussian,\n        cases = [\n            (q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(1.0)),) => 1.5 + log(2π) / 2,\n        ],\n        check_type_promotion = :exhaustive, check_nonallocating = true,\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:                          | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: average energy, exhaustively | \u001b[32m  24  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   24  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.8s\n\nThe promotion check shapes how a rule is written. The average energy computes log(2v * π), not log(2π * v): 2π is a Float64, which would turn a Float32 energy into a Float64 one, and the promoted runs would fail.\n\nThe tolerances are atol and rtol. Each is a number, or a dictionary from float type to number. By default, atol is 1e-4, 1e-6 and 1e-8 for Float32, Float64 and BigFloat, and rtol is 0. Every case also records the rule it selected, for the rule-coverage gate. Reading a failure shows what each check reports when it fails.","title":"What every case checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tables/#API","page":"Table tests","text":"The macros are the usual form. They report failures at the line of the table.\n\nThe functions behind them take the node and the target positionally. Their docstrings describe every keyword.\n\nAn expected value may carry more than the result.\n\nThe comparison the tables make, and the scratch poisoning, are public, for tests of your own.","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_message_update_rule","page":"Table tests","text":"@test_message_update_rule(node = ..., target = ..., cases = [inputs => expected, ...], ...)\n\nCheck the message rule of a node towards one of its interfaces against a table of cases, each the inputs of one call and the message it must return. It takes the arguments and keywords of test_message_update_rule, where they are described, all by name.\n\nThe macro takes keyword arguments only, written name = value, and passes them to test_message_update_rule; node and target are required, and a positional argument or a missing required keyword is an error when the macro expands. Every check is reported against the line of the macro call, so a failure points at the call that made it.\n\nExamples\n\n@test_message_update_rule(\n    node = NormalMeanVariance,\n    target = :out,\n    cases = [\n        (m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0),\n        (q = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = InverseGamma(3.0, 4.0)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 4 / 3),\n        (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 3.0), 0),\n    ],\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_message_update_rule"},{"category":"macro","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_marginal_update_rule","page":"Table tests","text":"@test_marginal_update_rule(node = ..., target = (:out, :μ), cases = [inputs => expected, ...], ...)\n\nCheck the marginal rule of a node for one of its clusters against a table of cases, each the inputs of one call and the joint marginal it must return. It takes the arguments and keywords of test_marginal_update_rule, where they are described, all by name.\n\nThe macro takes keyword arguments only, written name = value, and passes them to test_marginal_update_rule; node and target are required, and a positional argument or a missing required keyword is an error when the macro expands. Every check is reported against the line of the macro call, so a failure points at the call that made it.\n\nExamples\n\n@test_marginal_update_rule(\n    node = NormalMeanVariance,\n    target = (:out, :μ),\n    cases = [\n        (m = (out = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(2.0, 1.0)), q = (v = PointMass(1.0),)) => q_outμ,\n    ],\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_marginal_update_rule"},{"category":"macro","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_average_energy","page":"Table tests","text":"@test_average_energy(node = ..., cases = [inputs => expected, ...], ...)\n\nCheck the average energy of a node against a table of cases, each the marginals of one call and the energy it must return. It takes the arguments and keywords of test_average_energy, where they are described, all by name.\n\nThe macro takes keyword arguments only, written name = value, and passes them to test_average_energy; node is required, and a positional argument or a missing required keyword is an error when the macro expands. Every check is reported against the line of the macro call, so a failure points at the call that made it.\n\nExamples\n\n@test_average_energy(\n    node = NormalMeanVariance,\n    cases = [\n        (q = (out = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(1.0)),) => 1.5 + log(2π) / 2,\n    ],\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_average_energy"},{"category":"function","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_message_update_rule","page":"Table tests","text":"test_message_update_rule(node, target; cases, algorithm = default_algorithm(node), atol = nothing,\n    rtol = nothing, check_type_promotion = true, float_types = (Float32, Float64, BigFloat),\n    check_nonallocating = false) -> nothing\n\nCheck the message rule of node towards target against a table of cases, each the inputs of one call and the message it must return. @test_message_update_rule is the same check written with keywords, and reports failures at its own line.\n\nArguments\n\nnode: the node, as declared with @define_factor_node.\ntarget: the interface the message goes to: :out, or (:m, k) for member k of the group m.\n\nKeywords\n\ncases: the table, a vector of inputs => expected pairs. Required. inputs is a NamedTuple with any of these entries:\nm: the inbound messages, keyed by the interfaces' declared names: m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)). A group is a tuple of its members in order, with nothing for a member the rule does not take.\nq: the marginals of single interfaces, keyed the same way.\nclusters: the joint marginals of clusters, a tuple of pairs from the cluster's members, in interface order, to its joint: clusters = ((:out, :μ) => q_outμ,).\nctx: the RuleContext of services the rule reads. Default: an empty one. Its services are not checked: one the rule declares and ctx lacks reads as nothing inside the rule.\nlogscale: the log scales that arrived with the messages, keyed like m: logscale = (in = 0.5,), for a rule declared with reads_logscale = true.\nexpected is the message: a plain value, an ExpectedWithLogScale to check the log scale the rule declares as well, or an ExpectedWithAnnotations to check what it annotates.\nalgorithm: the algorithm value to run under. Default: default_algorithm(node). Under a DefaultAlgorithmExtension the extension's rules are selected, and the default's where it has none; an inherited rule runs with the value it was written for, as an engine runs it.\natol: the absolute tolerance, a number, or a Dict from float type to number, looked up by the float type of the expected value. Default: nothing, which is 1e-4 for Float32, 1e-6 for Float64, 1e-8 for BigFloat and 1e-6 for any other type; a Dict without an entry for a type falls back to the same values.\nrtol: the relative tolerance, in the same forms. Default: nothing, which is 0. A Dict without an entry for a type gives 0 for it.\ncheck_type_promotion: whether to run each case again with inputs of other float types. true (the default) runs it, for every type T in float_types, with all its inputs converted to T and, when it has more than one, with each input alone converted; :exhaustive converts every non-empty subset of the inputs instead; false skips it. An input is one entry of m, q or clusters, a group's tuple counting as one, converted with BayesBase.convert_paramfloattype; ctx and logscale stay as they are. The expected value is converted to the promoted float type of all the inputs, and the result must equal it, type included.\nfloat_types: the types promotion converts to. Default: (Float32, Float64, BigFloat).\ncheck_nonallocating: whether the rule must allocate nothing. Default: false. When true, the rule is run as an engine runs it: resolved, then called with execute_rule on a scratch built once and kept between calls, and, for an in-place rule, a buffer preallocated once. The call is measured with @allocated on its second run, after compilation, and must allocate 0 bytes.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\n\nChecks\n\nEach case makes these checks, each a Test assertion:\n\nA rule is found for the inputs and runs. If none is found, the case fails with the RuleNotFoundError text, which lists the closest rules; if the rule throws, the case fails with the exception and where it was thrown. Either way its other checks are skipped, and the table's other cases still run. The rule found is recorded as selected for check_rule_coverage.\nThe result equals the expected value by approximately_equal: same type, values within the tolerances.\nThe annotations an ExpectedWithAnnotations names, and the log scale an ExpectedWithLogScale gives.\nFor an in-place rule, rule! into a buffer from the rule's preallocate returns that buffer, and agrees with rule.\nFor a rule with scratch, two more runs on one scratch from the rule's scratch function, the first on the scratch as it is and the second after poison!, each into a fresh output, agree with the first result.\nWith check_nonallocating = true, the rule allocates nothing.\nWith check_type_promotion, every promoted run finds a rule and returns the promoted expected value, and a floating-point log scale of the promoted type when the case expects one. Annotations, rule! and scratch are checked on the case's own inputs only.\n\nA rule that throws in any of these runs, rule!, the scratch runs, the measured run or a promoted one, fails that check with the exception, and the others still run.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when check_type_promotion is not true, false or :exhaustive, when a case is not a Pair, or when a case's inputs have an entry other than m, q, clusters, ctx and logscale. A rule that is missing, wrong or throws is a test failure, not an error.\n\nExamples\n\ntest_message_update_rule(\n    NormalMeanVariance, :out;\n    cases = [\n        (m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0),\n        (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)),) => ExpectedWithLogScale(NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 3.0), 0),\n    ],\n)\n\nSee also test_marginal_update_rule, test_average_energy, check_rule_coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_message_update_rule"},{"category":"function","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_marginal_update_rule","page":"Table tests","text":"test_marginal_update_rule(node, target; cases, algorithm = default_algorithm(node), atol = nothing,\n    rtol = nothing, check_type_promotion = true, float_types = (Float32, Float64, BigFloat),\n    check_nonallocating = false) -> nothing\n\nCheck the marginal rule of node for the cluster target against a table of cases, each the inputs of one call and the joint marginal it must return. @test_marginal_update_rule is the same check written with keywords, and reports failures at its own line.\n\nArguments\n\nnode: the node, as declared with @define_factor_node.\ntarget: the cluster whose joint marginal the rule computes, its members in interface order: (:out, :μ), or (:out, (:T, 1)) with a group member.\n\nKeywords\n\ncases: the table, a vector of inputs => expected pairs. Required. inputs is a NamedTuple with any of these entries:\nm: the inbound messages, keyed by the interfaces' declared names: m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)). A group is a tuple of its members in order, with nothing for a member the rule does not take.\nq: the marginals of single interfaces, keyed the same way.\nclusters: the joint marginals of clusters, a tuple of pairs from the cluster's members, in interface order, to its joint: clusters = ((:out, :μ) => q_outμ,).\nctx: the RuleContext of services the rule reads. Default: an empty one. Its services are not checked: one the rule declares and ctx lacks reads as nothing inside the rule.\nlogscale: the log scales that arrived with the messages, keyed like m: logscale = (in = 0.5,), for a rule declared with reads_logscale = true.\nexpected is the joint marginal, a plain value or an ExpectedWithAnnotations; a cluster that factorises is expected as a FactorizedCluster. A marginal has no log scale, so an ExpectedWithLogScale fails.\nalgorithm: the algorithm value to run under. Default: default_algorithm(node). Under a DefaultAlgorithmExtension the extension's rules are selected, and the default's where it has none; an inherited rule runs with the value it was written for, as an engine runs it.\natol: the absolute tolerance, a number, or a Dict from float type to number, looked up by the float type of the expected value. Default: nothing, which is 1e-4 for Float32, 1e-6 for Float64, 1e-8 for BigFloat and 1e-6 for any other type; a Dict without an entry for a type falls back to the same values.\nrtol: the relative tolerance, in the same forms. Default: nothing, which is 0. A Dict without an entry for a type gives 0 for it.\ncheck_type_promotion: whether to run each case again with inputs of other float types. true (the default) runs it, for every type T in float_types, with all its inputs converted to T and, when it has more than one, with each input alone converted; :exhaustive converts every non-empty subset of the inputs instead; false skips it. An input is one entry of m, q or clusters, a group's tuple counting as one, converted with BayesBase.convert_paramfloattype; ctx and logscale stay as they are. The expected value is converted to the promoted float type of all the inputs, and the result must equal it, type included.\nfloat_types: the types promotion converts to. Default: (Float32, Float64, BigFloat).\ncheck_nonallocating: whether the rule must allocate nothing. Default: false. When true, the rule is run as an engine runs it: resolved, then called with execute_rule on a scratch built once and kept between calls, and, for an in-place rule, a buffer preallocated once. The call is measured with @allocated on its second run, after compilation, and must allocate 0 bytes.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\n\nChecks\n\nEach case makes these checks, each a Test assertion:\n\nA rule is found for the inputs and runs. If none is found, the case fails with the RuleNotFoundError text, which lists the closest rules; if the rule throws, the case fails with the exception and where it was thrown. Either way its other checks are skipped, and the table's other cases still run. The rule found is recorded as selected for check_rule_coverage.\nThe result equals the expected value by approximately_equal: same type, values within the tolerances.\nThe annotations an ExpectedWithAnnotations names, and the log scale an ExpectedWithLogScale gives.\nFor an in-place rule, rule! into a buffer from the rule's preallocate returns that buffer, and agrees with rule.\nFor a rule with scratch, two more runs on one scratch from the rule's scratch function, the first on the scratch as it is and the second after poison!, each into a fresh output, agree with the first result.\nWith check_nonallocating = true, the rule allocates nothing.\nWith check_type_promotion, every promoted run finds a rule and returns the promoted expected value, and a floating-point log scale of the promoted type when the case expects one. Annotations, rule! and scratch are checked on the case's own inputs only.\n\nA rule that throws in any of these runs, rule!, the scratch runs, the measured run or a promoted one, fails that check with the exception, and the others still run.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when check_type_promotion is not true, false or :exhaustive, when a case is not a Pair, or when a case's inputs have an entry other than m, q, clusters, ctx and logscale. A rule that is missing, wrong or throws is a test failure, not an error.\n\nSee also test_message_update_rule, test_average_energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_marginal_update_rule"},{"category":"function","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_average_energy","page":"Table tests","text":"test_average_energy(node; cases, algorithm = default_algorithm(node), atol = nothing,\n    rtol = nothing, check_type_promotion = true, float_types = (Float32, Float64, BigFloat),\n    check_nonallocating = false) -> nothing\n\nCheck the average energy of node against a table of cases, each the marginals of one call and the energy it must return. @test_average_energy is the same check written with keywords, and reports failures at its own line.\n\nArguments\n\nnode: the node, as declared with @define_factor_node.\n\nKeywords\n\ncases: the table, a vector of inputs => expected pairs. Required. inputs is a NamedTuple with any of these entries:\nm: the inbound messages, keyed by the interfaces' declared names: m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), v = PointMass(2.0)). A group is a tuple of its members in order, with nothing for a member the rule does not take.\nq: the marginals of single interfaces, keyed the same way.\nclusters: the joint marginals of clusters, a tuple of pairs from the cluster's members, in interface order, to its joint: clusters = ((:out, :μ) => q_outμ,).\nctx: the RuleContext of services the rule reads. Default: an empty one. Its services are not checked: one the rule declares and ctx lacks reads as nothing inside the rule.\nlogscale: the log scales that arrived with the messages, keyed like m: logscale = (in = 0.5,), for a rule declared with reads_logscale = true.\nAn average energy reads marginals, q and clusters, one per cluster of the factorisation. expected is the energy, a number, or an ExpectedWithAnnotations; an average energy has no log scale, so an ExpectedWithLogScale fails.\nalgorithm: the algorithm value to run under. Default: default_algorithm(node). Under a DefaultAlgorithmExtension the extension's rules are selected, and the default's where it has none; an inherited rule runs with the value it was written for, as an engine runs it.\natol: the absolute tolerance, a number, or a Dict from float type to number, looked up by the float type of the expected value. Default: nothing, which is 1e-4 for Float32, 1e-6 for Float64, 1e-8 for BigFloat and 1e-6 for any other type; a Dict without an entry for a type falls back to the same values.\nrtol: the relative tolerance, in the same forms. Default: nothing, which is 0. A Dict without an entry for a type gives 0 for it.\ncheck_type_promotion: whether to run each case again with inputs of other float types. true (the default) runs it, for every type T in float_types, with all its inputs converted to T and, when it has more than one, with each input alone converted; :exhaustive converts every non-empty subset of the inputs instead; false skips it. An input is one entry of m, q or clusters, a group's tuple counting as one, converted with BayesBase.convert_paramfloattype; ctx and logscale stay as they are. The expected value is converted to the promoted float type of all the inputs, and the result must equal it, type included.\nfloat_types: the types promotion converts to. Default: (Float32, Float64, BigFloat).\ncheck_nonallocating: whether the rule must allocate nothing. Default: false. When true, the rule is run as an engine runs it: resolved, then called with execute_rule on a scratch built once and kept between calls, and, for an in-place rule, a buffer preallocated once. The call is measured with @allocated on its second run, after compilation, and must allocate 0 bytes.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\n\nChecks\n\nEach case makes these checks, each a Test assertion:\n\nA rule is found for the inputs and runs. If none is found, the case fails with the RuleNotFoundError text, which lists the closest rules; if the rule throws, the case fails with the exception and where it was thrown. Either way its other checks are skipped, and the table's other cases still run. The rule found is recorded as selected for check_rule_coverage.\nThe result equals the expected value by approximately_equal: same type, values within the tolerances.\nThe annotations an ExpectedWithAnnotations names, and the log scale an ExpectedWithLogScale gives.\nFor an in-place rule, rule! into a buffer from the rule's preallocate returns that buffer, and agrees with rule.\nFor a rule with scratch, two more runs on one scratch from the rule's scratch function, the first on the scratch as it is and the second after poison!, each into a fresh output, agree with the first result.\nWith check_nonallocating = true, the rule allocates nothing.\nWith check_type_promotion, every promoted run finds a rule and returns the promoted expected value, and a floating-point log scale of the promoted type when the case expects one. Annotations, rule! and scratch are checked on the case's own inputs only.\n\nA rule that throws in any of these runs, rule!, the scratch runs, the measured run or a promoted one, fails that check with the exception, and the others still run.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when check_type_promotion is not true, false or :exhaustive, when a case is not a Pair, or when a case's inputs have an entry other than m, q, clusters, ctx and logscale. A rule that is missing, wrong or throws is a test failure, not an error.\n\nSee also test_message_update_rule, test_marginal_update_rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_average_energy"},{"category":"type","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.ExpectedWithLogScale","page":"Table tests","text":"ExpectedWithLogScale(value, logscale::Real)\n\nThe expected message of a table case together with the log scale its rule must declare: ExpectedWithLogScale(Beta(2.0, 1.0), -log(2)). For message rules only: marginal rules and average energies have no log scale, and a case expecting one fails.\n\nThe log scale must be a number, so a rule that declares none fails, and it is compared with logscale within the table's tolerances for the float type of logscale. On every promoted run of the case (see check_type_promotion), a floating-point log scale must also be of the inputs' promoted float type, as the message is: a computed log scale follows the inputs' precision. A declared integer or irrational constant, logscale = 0, passes that check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.ExpectedWithLogScale"},{"category":"type","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.ExpectedWithAnnotations","page":"Table tests","text":"ExpectedWithAnnotations(value; annotations...)\nExpectedWithAnnotations(value, annotations::NamedTuple)\n\nThe expected result of a table case together with the annotations the rule must write: ExpectedWithAnnotations(NormalMeanVariance(0.0, 1.0); input_count = 2). The result is compared as a plain expected value is; each annotation is looked up by name among those the rule wrote with annotate!, must be present, and is compared by value within the table's tolerances, not by type. Annotations are checked on the case's own inputs, not on its promoted runs.\n\nThrows\n\nArgumentError when logscale is among the annotations: a log scale is part of the message, not an annotation, and is expected with ExpectedWithLogScale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.ExpectedWithAnnotations"},{"category":"function","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.approximately_equal","page":"Table tests","text":"approximately_equal(a, b; atol, rtol) -> Bool\n\nWhether two rule outputs agree, the comparison every table case, in-place and scratch check makes. The types must match exactly: a Normal{Float32} never equals a Normal{Float64}. Then the values are compared by what they hold:\n\nnumbers by isapprox, and arrays, tuples and named tuples (with the same keys) element by element;\na PointMass by its location, and any other Distribution by its params;\na FactorizedCluster by its blocks, which must be the same, and then its components;\nnothing equals nothing;\nany other immutable struct with fields field by field, such as ExponentialFamily's JointNormal, which is not a Distribution; everything else by ==.\n\nKeywords\n\natol, rtol: the absolute and relative tolerances of isapprox, both required.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.approximately_equal(Normal(0.0, 1.0), Normal(1e-8, 1.0); atol = 1e-6, rtol = 0)\ntrue\n\njulia> MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.approximately_equal(Normal(0.0, 1.0), Normal(0.0f0, 1.0f0); atol = 1e-6, rtol = 0)\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.approximately_equal"},{"category":"function","location":"tables/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.poison!","page":"Table tests","text":"poison!(scratch) -> scratch\n\nOverwrite a rule's scratch with values no correct computation reads: NaN in every floating-point array, found through arrays of arrays, tuples and named tuples; any other storage is left as it is. Table tests run a rule that has scratch once more after poisoning its scratch, and the result must not change: a rule that reads its scratch before writing it fails. A package whose scratch holds another kind of storage adds a method.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> scratch = (work = zeros(2), counts = [1, 2]);\n\njulia> MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.poison!(scratch) === scratch\ntrue\n\njulia> all(isnan, scratch.work), scratch.counts\n(true, [1, 2])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.poison!"},{"category":"section","location":"derivatives/#Derivatives","page":"Derivatives","text":"A model may be differentiated, to fit hyperparameters or to run a gradient-based sampler. Its derivatives then pass through its rules. @test_rule_derivatives checks that they do. It builds the rule's inputs from a parameter θ, reduces the rule's output to a number with summary, and compares ForwardDiff's derivative with a central finite difference.\n\nThe rule under test is the belief propagation rule towards out of the Gaussian node of the first tutorial:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\nA scalar θ sets the mean of the message on μ and the variance v. A vector θ sets the mean and the variance of the message on μ, and the check compares the gradient:\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: derivatives\" begin\n    @test_rule_derivatives(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        inputs = θ -> (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(θ, 1.0), v = PointMass(θ^2)),),\n        at = 1.5, summary = d -> mean(d) + var(d),\n    )\n    @test_rule_derivatives(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        inputs = θ -> (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(θ[1], θ[2]), v = PointMass(0.5)),),\n        at = [1.0, 2.0], summary = var,\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary:         | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: derivatives | \u001b[32m   2  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m    2  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m1.4s\n\nA rule that converts its inputs to Float64, or strips the dual part of a number, gives a wrong or zero derivative and fails. An in-place rule is checked twice, through rule and through rule!. Reading a failure shows what a failing check reports.","title":"Derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"derivatives/#API","page":"Derivatives","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"macro","location":"derivatives/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_rule_derivatives","page":"Derivatives","text":"@test_rule_derivatives(node = ..., target = ..., inputs = θ -> (m = (...),), at = ..., ...)\n\nCheck that derivatives propagate through the message rule of a node towards one of its interfaces, by ForwardDiff against a central finite difference. It takes the arguments and keywords of test_rule_derivatives, where they are described, all by name.\n\nThe macro takes keyword arguments only, written name = value, and passes them to test_rule_derivatives; node and target are required, and a positional argument or a missing required keyword is an error when the macro expands. Every check is reported against the line of the macro call, so a failure points at the call that made it.\n\nExamples\n\n@test_rule_derivatives(\n    node = NormalMeanVariance, target = :out,\n    inputs = θ -> (m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(θ, 1.0), v = PointMass(θ^2)),),\n    at = 1.5, summary = d -> mean(d) + var(d),\n)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.@test_rule_derivatives"},{"category":"function","location":"derivatives/#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_rule_derivatives","page":"Derivatives","text":"test_rule_derivatives(node, target; inputs, at, summary = mean, algorithm = default_algorithm(node),\n    step = 1e-6, rtol = 1e-5, atol = 1e-8) -> nothing\n\nCheck that derivatives propagate through the message rule of node towards target: the derivative of θ -> summary(rule(inputs(θ))) by ForwardDiff must agree with a central finite difference at θ = at. A rule that drops the dual part of its inputs fails the comparison. One that cannot take dual numbers, or throws at a perturbed θ, fails as rule_runs with the exception; no rule for the inputs, at at or at a dual or perturbed θ, fails as rule_found with the near misses. Nothing is thrown for a rule, and the checks after it still run. @test_rule_derivatives is the same check written with keywords, and reports failures at its own line.\n\nThe rule is resolved for the inputs at each θ, and the one found at at is recorded as selected for check_rule_coverage. An in-place rule is checked a second time through rule!, into a buffer its preallocate makes from the dual inputs, so both paths are covered. The rule runs with an empty RuleContext, and its log scale is not checked.\n\nArguments\n\nnode: the node, as declared with @define_factor_node.\ntarget: the interface the message goes to: :out, or (:m, k) for member k of the group m.\n\nKeywords\n\ninputs: a function of θ returning a NamedTuple with the rule's inbound messages m and marginals q: θ -> (m = (out = Normal(θ, 2.0), σ = PointMass(θ^2)),). Required. Joint marginals of clusters are not supported.\nat: the point to differentiate at, a number or a vector. Required.\nsummary: a function from the rule's output to a number, whose derivative is compared. Default: mean.\nalgorithm: the algorithm value to run under. Default: default_algorithm(node).\nstep: the finite-difference step. Default: 1e-6.\nrtol, atol: the tolerances of the comparison, element by element for a vector at. Defaults: 1e-5 and 1e-8.\nsource: the line checks are reported against. Default: LineNumberNode(0, :unknown); the macro form passes its own line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils.test_rule_derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils","page":"Overview","text":"A rule package defines factor nodes and their rules with MessagePassingRulesBase. This package tests those rules on their own, the way they are defined, without building a graph. It is a test dependency only: nothing needs it at run time.\n\nThis site is for rule authors, who test the rules of a package of their own. It assumes you have written a rule, as MessagePassingRulesBase's Your first node does.","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-test","page":"Overview","text":"A node with one rule, the belief propagation message towards the output of a normal distribution with a known variance, and a table with one case for it:\n\nusing MessagePassingRulesBase, MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\nusing BayesBase, ExponentialFamily, Distributions, Test\n\nstruct Gaussian end   # out ~ Normal(μ, v)\nGaussian(μ, v) = NormalMeanVariance(μ, v)\n\n@define_factor_node(node = Gaussian, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :v])\n\n@define_message_update_rule(\n    node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n    args = (m[:μ]::NormalMeanVariance, m[:v]::PointMass),\n    logscale = 0,\n    body = (args) -> NormalMeanVariance(mean(args.m[:μ]), var(args.m[:μ]) + mean(args.m[:v])),\n)\n\n@testset \"Gaussian: out\" begin\n    @test_message_update_rule(\n        node = Gaussian, target = :out,\n        cases = [(m = (μ = NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.0), v = PointMass(0.5)),) => NormalMeanVariance(1.0, 2.5)],\n    )\nend\n\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTest Summary: | \u001b[22m\u001b[32m\u001b[1mPass  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[36m\u001b[1mTotal  \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[0m\u001b[1mTime\u001b[22m\nGaussian: out | \u001b[32m  11  \u001b[39m\u001b[36m   11  \u001b[39m\u001b[0m0.3s\n\nThe case gives the rule its inbound messages and the message it must return. The summary counts eleven checks for the one case: the table found the rule, compared its result with the expected one, and ran the case again with its inputs converted to other float types. Testing your first rule explains each check.\n\ninfo: Where these rules run\nThe ReactiveMP engine runs these rules on a factor graph, which RxInfer builds from a model. The tests on this site call the rules directly, never inside a model.","title":"A first test"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-site","page":"Overview","text":"Tutorials: Testing your first rule writes tables for a node's belief propagation and variational rules, and reads what a failing case reports. Testing a rule package adds verification against the node, derivative checks and the rule-coverage gate, as every rule package's suite has them.\nTable tests: every check a case makes, tables for marginal rules and average energies, and the tolerances.\nVerification against the node: a message rule against the node's log-density, integrated numerically, and what it can integrate.\nDerivatives: automatic differentiation through a rule, against finite differences.\nThe rule-coverage gate: every rule selected by some test, and how a suite runs the gate.\nReading a failure: what each failing check reports, and what to do about it.","title":"The site"},{"category":"module","location":"#MessagePassingRulesTestUtils","page":"Overview","text":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils\n\nTools for testing message passing rules on their own, without a graph, the way a rule package defines them with @define_message_update_rule and its siblings. Every check is a Test assertion, reported against the line of the test that made it, so the tools go inside ordinary @testsets or @testitems.\n\nA rule package's suite uses them in four layers:\n\nTable tests, @test_message_update_rule, @test_marginal_update_rule and @test_average_energy: each case gives a rule's inputs and the result it must return. Every case is also run with its inputs converted to other float types, through rule! for an in-place rule and on a poisoned scratch for a rule with scratch.\nVerification against the node, @verify_message_update_rule: a message rule's output, and its log scale, against the node's own log-density, integrated numerically.\nDerivative checks, @test_rule_derivatives: automatic differentiation through a rule against finite differences.\nThe rule-coverage gate, check_rule_coverage: after an unfiltered run of the suite, every rule the package defines must have been selected by some test.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Gauss  # a toy node: out ~ Normal(μ, σ)\n           μ::Float64\n           σ::Float64\n       end\n\njulia> BayesBase.logpdf(d::Gauss, x) = logpdf(Normal(d.μ, d.σ), x);\n\njulia> @define_factor_node(node = Gauss, type = Stochastic, interfaces = [:out, :μ, :σ]);\n\njulia> @define_message_update_rule(\n           node = Gauss, target = :out, args = (m[:μ]::PointMass, m[:σ]::PointMass),\n           body = (args) -> Normal(mean(args.m[:μ]), mean(args.m[:σ])),\n       );\n\njulia> @test_message_update_rule(\n           node = Gauss, target = :out,\n           cases = [(m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), σ = PointMass(2.0)),) => Normal(1.0, 2.0)],\n       )\n\njulia> @verify_message_update_rule(node = Gauss, target = :out, m = (μ = PointMass(1.0), σ = PointMass(2.0)));\n\njulia> isempty(check_rule_coverage(@__MODULE__))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"MessagePassingRulesTestUtils"}]
}
